(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-security",{"k8s-security/threat-model-and-the-layers-of-cluster-security":{"html":"\u003cp>Security work is easier when it starts from how attacks actually proceed. A cluster has several layers, each with its own entry points, and the strongest control is usually the one that stops the attacker at the first step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The layers: cloud, cluster, container and code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common attack paths in Kubernetes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assets and trust boundaries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mapping controls to attack steps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where the CKS domains fit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-layers\">The layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The cloud native security model describes four layers, cloud or infrastructure, cluster, container and code. Each layer builds on the one below, so a weakness lower down undermines controls above it. A hardened container on a node whose kubelet accepts anonymous requests is still exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"attack-paths\">Attack paths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A typical path begins with a vulnerable application or a leaked credential. The attacker gets code execution in a pod, reads the mounted service account token, calls the API server, and tries to escalate through permissive RBAC, a privileged pod, a host mount or the cloud metadata service. From a node they can reach other pods, secrets and cloud credentials. Another path begins with an exposed dashboard, kubelet or etcd port.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"assets-and-boundaries\">Assets and boundaries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List what is valuable: secrets, customer data, the ability to run code on nodes, cloud credentials, the CI pipeline and the images it produces. Mark trust boundaries: internet to ingress, pod to pod across namespaces, workload to API server, node to cloud, and build to runtime. Controls belong at those boundaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mapping-controls\">Mapping controls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each step in a path, name the control that stops or detects it. Image scanning and minimal images reduce the chance of the first foothold. Dropping unnecessary capabilities and running as non-root limits what code can do. Disabling token automount denies the API foothold. Least privilege RBAC limits escalation. Network policies and metadata blocking limit movement. Audit logs and runtime detection find what slipped through.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"path = [\n    (&quot;exploit app&quot;, &quot;image scan, patching&quot;),\n    (&quot;read service account token&quot;, &quot;automount off, short lived tokens&quot;),\n    (&quot;call API server&quot;, &quot;least privilege RBAC&quot;),\n    (&quot;escape to node&quot;, &quot;pod security restricted, seccomp, AppArmor&quot;),\n    (&quot;move sideways&quot;, &quot;network policy, metadata block&quot;),\n    (&quot;hide&quot;, &quot;audit logs, runtime detection&quot;),\n]\nfor step, control in path:\n    print(step, &quot;-&gt;&quot;, control)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">path = [\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exploit app&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image scan, patching&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read service account token&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;automount off, short lived tokens&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;call API server&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;least privilege RBAC&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;escape to node&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod security restricted, seccomp, AppArmor&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;move sideways&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;network policy, metadata block&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hide&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;audit logs, runtime detection&quot;\u003c/span>),\n]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> step, control \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> path:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(step, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-&gt;&quot;\u003c/span>, control)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-this-maps-to-cks\">How this maps to CKS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CKS exam domains line up with the layers: cluster setup and hardening, system hardening, minimising microservice vulnerabilities, supply chain security and monitoring and runtime security. This module goes deep on the reasoning, and the exam practice module drills the tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"code&gt;pod;pod&gt;api;pod&gt;node;node&gt;cloud\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Threat model and the layers of cluster security\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"code\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod and container\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node and host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cloud\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud credentials\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application code to Pod and container; 2, Pod and container to API server; 3, Pod and container to Node and host; 4, Node and host to Cloud credentials.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"code\">\u003cdt>Application code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Entry point.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Known vulnerability.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod and container \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Foothold.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Root, caps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Escalation target.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Broad RBAC.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node and host \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Escape target.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Privileged pod.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cloud\">\u003cdt>Cloud credentials \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Wider impact.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Metadata reachable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-from-one-vulnerable-pod-to-the-cloud-account\">Incident: From one vulnerable pod to the cloud account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A compromised web pod ended with access to a cloud storage bucket.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-1 -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace\nkubectl exec web-1 -- curl -s -m 2 http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- curl -s -m 2 http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">node-role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pod could read its service account token and also reach the cloud metadata endpoint, which returned the node role credentials. The attacker used those to read a storage bucket. Disabling token automount for pods that do not need it, blocking the metadata address with network policy and using workload identity instead of node roles would each have cut the path. Layers of controls matter because the first one often fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Securing the cluster but not the cloud role\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving default service account tokens mounted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating the metadata endpoint as harmless\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only scanning images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not writing down the attack paths you defend\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-layers","text":"The layers"},{"id":"attack-paths","text":"Attack paths"},{"id":"assets-and-boundaries","text":"Assets and boundaries"},{"id":"mapping-controls","text":"Mapping controls"},{"id":"how-this-maps-to-cks","text":"How this maps to CKS"},{"id":"incident-from-one-vulnerable-pod-to-the-cloud-account","text":"Incident: From one vulnerable pod to the cloud account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes security overview","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Security checklist","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/security-checklist/"}],"text":"Security work is easier when it starts from how attacks actually proceed. A cluster has several layers, each with its own entry points, and the strongest control is usually the one that stops the attacker at the first step. What you will learn The layers: cloud, cluster, container and code Common attack paths in Kubernetes Assets and trust boundaries Mapping controls to attack steps Where the CKS domains fit The layers The cloud native security model describes four layers, cloud or infrastructure, cluster, container and code. Each layer builds on the one below, so a weakness lower down undermines controls above it. A hardened container on a node whose kubelet accepts anonymous requests is still exposed. Attack paths A typical path begins with a vulnerable application or a leaked credential. The attacker gets code execution in a pod, reads the mounted service account token, calls the API server, and tries to escalate through permissive RBAC, a privileged pod, a host mount or the cloud metadata service. From a node they can reach other pods, secrets and cloud credentials. Another path begins with an exposed dashboard, kubelet or etcd port. Assets and boundaries List what is valuable: secrets, customer data, the ability to run code on nodes, cloud credentials, the CI pipeline and the images it produces. Mark trust boundaries: internet to ingress, pod to pod across namespaces, workload to API server, node to cloud, and build to runtime. Controls belong at those boundaries. Mapping controls For each step in a path, name the control that stops or detects it. Image scanning and minimal images reduce the chance of the first foothold. Dropping unnecessary capabilities and running as non-root limits what code can do. Disabling token automount denies the API foothold. Least privilege RBAC limits escalation. Network policies and metadata blocking limit movement. Audit logs and runtime detection find what slipped through. path = [ ( \"exploit app\" , \"image scan, patching\" ), ( \"read service account token\" , \"automount off, short lived tokens\" ), ( \"call API server\" , \"least privilege RBAC\" ), ( \"escape to node\" , \"pod security restricted, seccomp, AppArmor\" ), ( \"move sideways\" , \"network policy, metadata block\" ), ( \"hide\" , \"audit logs, runtime detection\" ), ] for step, control in path: print (step, \"->\" , control) How this maps to CKS The CKS exam domains line up with the layers: cluster setup and hardening, system hardening, minimising microservice vulnerabilities, supply chain security and monitoring and runtime security. This module goes deep on the reasoning, and the exam practice module drills the tasks. Threat model and the layers of cluster security Flow in order: 1, Application code to Pod and container; 2, Pod and container to API server; 3, Pod and container to Node and host; 4, Node and host to Cloud credentials. Outside party Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application code Outside party What it does: Entry point. If it fails or is weak: Known vulnerability. Pod and container Step What it does: Foothold. If it fails or is weak: Root, caps. API server Security What it does: Escalation target. If it fails or is weak: Broad RBAC. Node and host Security What it does: Escape target. If it fails or is weak: Privileged pod. Cloud credentials Outside party What it does: Wider impact. If it fails or is weak: Metadata reachable. Incident: From one vulnerable pod to the cloud account Incident A compromised web pod ended with access to a cloud storage bucket. $ kubectl exec web-1 -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace shop $ kubectl exec web-1 -- curl -s -m 2 http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ node-role (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The pod could read its service account token and also reach the cloud metadata endpoint, which returned the node role credentials. Th"},"k8s-security/authentication-service-accounts-and-token-security":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes has no built-in user database. Humans arrive with certificates or tokens from an identity provider, and workloads arrive as service accounts. Most real incidents involve a credential that was too powerful, too long lived or in the wrong place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How users are authenticated: certificates, OIDC and webhooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service accounts and the tokens pods receive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bound, audience scoped, short lived tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning off automount and avoiding default accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Anonymous access and kubeconfig hygiene\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"users\">Users\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Users are not objects in the API. A client certificate signed by the cluster CA gives a user name from its common name and groups from its organisation. This is simple, but certificates cannot be revoked individually, so avoid them for people. OpenID Connect integrates with an identity provider, giving short lived tokens and central offboarding, and is the usual choice. Static token files and basic auth are legacy and should be off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-accounts\">Service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A ServiceAccount is the identity of a workload. Each namespace has a default one. Pods use it unless told otherwise. Current clusters issue projected tokens: they are bound to the pod, expire in about an hour by default, are refreshed by the kubelet and carry an audience. Older long lived secret based tokens exist only if created deliberately, and should be avoided.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reducing-exposure\">Reducing exposure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most pods never call the API server, so they should not carry a token. Set \u003ccode>automountServiceAccountToken: false\u003c/code> on the service account or the pod. Create dedicated service accounts per workload with only the roles they need, never rely on the default account, and never bind powerful roles to it. Where a token is needed for another system, request an audience scoped projected token.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: reporter\n  namespace: shop\nautomountServiceAccountToken: false\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ServiceAccount\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reporter\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automountServiceAccountToken:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-what-is-exposed\">Check what is exposed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get sa -A\nkubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop\nkubectl get secrets -A --field-selector type=kubernetes.io/service-account-token\nkubectl create token reporter -n shop --duration=10m\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get sa -A\nkubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop\nkubectl get secrets -A --field-selector \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=kubernetes.io/service-account-token\nkubectl create token reporter -n shop --duration=10m\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last command creates a short lived token for testing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"anonymous-access-and-kubeconfigs\">Anonymous access and kubeconfigs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>--anonymous-auth=false\u003c/code> on the API server unless a health check needs it, and on the kubelet. Treat kubeconfig files as credentials: keep them out of repositories, give each person their own, prefer short lived exec plugins from the cloud or OIDC, and rotate admin credentials after staff changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"user&gt;api;sa&gt;tok;tok&gt;api\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Authentication, service accounts and token security\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Human user\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sa\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ServiceAccount\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tok\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Projected token\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Human user to API server; 2, ServiceAccount to Projected token; 3, Projected token to API server.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Human user \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> OIDC or certificate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared admin config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sa\">\u003cdt>ServiceAccount \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Workload identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Default account.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tok\">\u003cdt>Projected token \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bound and short lived.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long lived secret.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Authenticates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Anonymous on.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-token-in-a-public-repository\">Incident: A token in a public repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer committed a kubeconfig with a long lived admin credential.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth whoami\nkubectl get secrets -A --field-selector type=kubernetes.io/service-account-token | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">whoami\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ATTRIBUTE   VALUE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Username    kubernetes-admin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Groups      [system:masters system:authenticated]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get secrets -A --field-selector \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=kubernetes.io/service-account-token | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The committed file held a client certificate in the system:masters group, which cannot be revoked without replacing the CA. Anyone who copied it had permanent cluster admin. The team rotated the cluster credentials, moved people to OIDC with short lived tokens, deleted legacy token secrets and added secret scanning to the repository. Prefer identities that can be revoked and that expire.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using client certificates for people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving automountServiceAccountToken on by default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Binding roles to the default service account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping legacy long lived token secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Committing kubeconfig files\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"users","text":"Users"},{"id":"service-accounts","text":"Service accounts"},{"id":"reducing-exposure","text":"Reducing exposure"},{"id":"check-what-is-exposed","text":"Check what is exposed"},{"id":"anonymous-access-and-kubeconfigs","text":"Anonymous access and kubeconfigs"},{"id":"incident-a-token-in-a-public-repository","text":"Incident: A token in a public repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Authenticating","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authentication/"},{"t":"Service accounts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"}],"text":"Kubernetes has no built-in user database. Humans arrive with certificates or tokens from an identity provider, and workloads arrive as service accounts. Most real incidents involve a credential that was too powerful, too long lived or in the wrong place. What you will learn How users are authenticated: certificates, OIDC and webhooks Service accounts and the tokens pods receive Bound, audience scoped, short lived tokens Turning off automount and avoiding default accounts Anonymous access and kubeconfig hygiene Users Users are not objects in the API. A client certificate signed by the cluster CA gives a user name from its common name and groups from its organisation. This is simple, but certificates cannot be revoked individually, so avoid them for people. OpenID Connect integrates with an identity provider, giving short lived tokens and central offboarding, and is the usual choice. Static token files and basic auth are legacy and should be off. Service accounts A ServiceAccount is the identity of a workload. Each namespace has a default one. Pods use it unless told otherwise. Current clusters issue projected tokens: they are bound to the pod, expire in about an hour by default, are refreshed by the kubelet and carry an audience. Older long lived secret based tokens exist only if created deliberately, and should be avoided. Reducing exposure Most pods never call the API server, so they should not carry a token. Set automountServiceAccountToken: false on the service account or the pod. Create dedicated service accounts per workload with only the roles they need, never rely on the default account, and never bind powerful roles to it. Where a token is needed for another system, request an audience scoped projected token. apiVersion: v1 kind: ServiceAccount metadata: name: reporter namespace: shop automountServiceAccountToken: false Check what is exposed kubectl get sa -A kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop kubectl get secrets -A --field-selector type =kubernetes.io/service-account-token kubectl create token reporter -n shop --duration=10m The last command creates a short lived token for testing. Anonymous access and kubeconfigs Set --anonymous-auth=false on the API server unless a health check needs it, and on the kubelet. Treat kubeconfig files as credentials: keep them out of repositories, give each person their own, prefer short lived exec plugins from the cloud or OIDC, and rotate admin credentials after staff changes. Authentication, service accounts and token security Flow in order: 1, Human user to API server; 2, ServiceAccount to Projected token; 3, Projected token to API server. Outside party Security Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Human user Outside party What it does: OIDC or certificate. If it fails or is weak: Shared admin config. ServiceAccount Security What it does: Workload identity. If it fails or is weak: Default account. Projected token Security What it does: Bound and short lived. If it fails or is weak: Long lived secret. API server Service What it does: Authenticates. If it fails or is weak: Anonymous on. Incident: A token in a public repository Incident A developer committed a kubeconfig with a long lived admin credential. $ kubectl auth whoami ATTRIBUTE VALUE Username kubernetes-admin Groups [system:masters system:authenticated] $ kubectl get secrets -A --field-selector type =kubernetes.io/service-account-token | wc -l 14 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The committed file held a client certificate in the system:masters group, which cannot be revoked without replacing the CA. Anyone who copied it had permanent cluster admin. The team rotated the cluster credentials, moved people to OIDC with short lived tokens, deleted legacy token secrets and added secret scanning to the repository. Prefer identities that can be revoked and that expire. Common mistakes Usin"},"k8s-security/rbac-design-least-privilege-and-auditing":{"html":"\u003cp>RBAC is simple to write and easy to over-grant. A few permissions are effectively cluster admin even though they look harmless, and reviewing roles for them is one of the highest value security tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The RBAC objects and how they combine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patterns: narrow roles, namespaced bindings, aggregated roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dangerous permissions and privilege escalation paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Auditing with can-i, who-can tools and the audit log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Process for requesting and reviewing access\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objects\">Objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Role grants permissions within a namespace, and a ClusterRole grants cluster wide or reusable permissions. A RoleBinding binds a role or cluster role to subjects within one namespace, and a ClusterRoleBinding does so for the whole cluster. Binding a ClusterRole with a RoleBinding is a good pattern: define the permission set once and grant it per namespace. Rules list API groups, resources, verbs and optionally resource names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"design\">Design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start from what the job needs and grant the verbs and resources for it, not a wildcard. Prefer groups from your identity provider as subjects over individual names. Use one namespace per team and bind built-in roles such as view and edit there. Keep admin and cluster-admin for a small set of break-glass accounts, with the use audited.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n shop\nkubectl create rolebinding jane-reads --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n shop\nkubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as jane\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n shop\nkubectl create rolebinding jane-reads --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n shop\nkubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as jane\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dangerous-permissions\">Dangerous permissions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some permissions allow escalation. The verbs escalate and bind on roles let someone grant themselves more. Creating pods in a namespace lets a user mount any secret there, run privileged containers if policy allows, or use any service account in the namespace. Reading secrets exposes credentials. Access to \u003ccode>pods/exec\u003c/code> gives a shell in workloads. Impersonation lets a subject act as another. Wildcards in verbs or resources, and any permission on \u003ccode>nodes/proxy\u003c/code>, deserve special review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"auditing\">Auditing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List who can do something with \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i --list --as\u003c/code>, or a plugin such as kubectl-who-can or rbac-tool to answer &quot;who can create pods&quot; across the cluster. Review ClusterRoleBindings to cluster-admin and system:masters regularly. The audit log shows which identities actually used permissions, which helps remove unused ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | grep -B2 -A8 cluster-admin | head -40\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | grep -B2 -A8 cluster-admin | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"process\">Process\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Request access through a reviewed change in version control, grant it with an expiry where possible and remove it when the need ends. Review roles each quarter. Treat a new role that includes secrets, exec, escalate, bind or wildcards as needing a second reviewer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"role&gt;bind;bind&gt;subj;subj&gt;aud\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">RBAC design, least privilege and auditing\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Role or ClusterRole\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bind\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Binding\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"subj\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Group or user\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Role or ClusterRole to Binding; 2, Binding to Group or user; 3, Group or user to Audit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>Role or ClusterRole \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Defines permissions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wildcards.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bind\">\u003cdt>Binding \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Grants to subjects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cluster wide by mistake.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"subj\">\u003cdt>Group or user \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receives access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Individuals.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finds excess.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never reviewed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-read-only-role-that-could-escalate\">Incident: The read-only role that could escalate\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A role called viewer turned out to let its holders become administrators.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get clusterrole viewer -o jsonpath='{.rules}'\nkubectl auth can-i create pods -n kube-system --as viewer-user\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get clusterrole viewer -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.rules}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[{&quot;apiGroups&quot;:[&quot;*&quot;],&quot;resources&quot;:[&quot;pods&quot;,&quot;secrets&quot;],&quot;verbs&quot;:[&quot;get&quot;,&quot;list&quot;,&quot;create&quot;]}]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i create pods -n kube-system --as viewer-user\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">yes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role allowed create on pods and get on secrets across the cluster, so a holder could start a pod in any namespace, mount any secret or use any service account. The name hid the risk. Replacing it with a narrow role bound only in specific namespaces, and reviewing roles for create on pods, secrets access, exec, escalate and bind, removed the path.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wildcards in verbs or resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting create pods without considering what pods can mount\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using ClusterRoleBindings where RoleBindings suffice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Binding to individuals instead of groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No periodic review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"objects","text":"Objects"},{"id":"design","text":"Design"},{"id":"dangerous-permissions","text":"Dangerous permissions"},{"id":"auditing","text":"Auditing"},{"id":"process","text":"Process"},{"id":"incident-the-read-only-role-that-could-escalate","text":"Incident: The read-only role that could escalate"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using RBAC authorization","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"},{"t":"RBAC good practices","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/rbac-good-practices/"}],"text":"RBAC is simple to write and easy to over-grant. A few permissions are effectively cluster admin even though they look harmless, and reviewing roles for them is one of the highest value security tasks. What you will learn The RBAC objects and how they combine Patterns: narrow roles, namespaced bindings, aggregated roles Dangerous permissions and privilege escalation paths Auditing with can-i, who-can tools and the audit log Process for requesting and reviewing access Objects A Role grants permissions within a namespace, and a ClusterRole grants cluster wide or reusable permissions. A RoleBinding binds a role or cluster role to subjects within one namespace, and a ClusterRoleBinding does so for the whole cluster. Binding a ClusterRole with a RoleBinding is a good pattern: define the permission set once and grant it per namespace. Rules list API groups, resources, verbs and optionally resource names. Design Start from what the job needs and grant the verbs and resources for it, not a wildcard. Prefer groups from your identity provider as subjects over individual names. Use one namespace per team and bind built-in roles such as view and edit there. Keep admin and cluster-admin for a small set of break-glass accounts, with the use audited. kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n shop kubectl create rolebinding jane-reads --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n shop kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as jane Dangerous permissions Some permissions allow escalation. The verbs escalate and bind on roles let someone grant themselves more. Creating pods in a namespace lets a user mount any secret there, run privileged containers if policy allows, or use any service account in the namespace. Reading secrets exposes credentials. Access to pods/exec gives a shell in workloads. Impersonation lets a subject act as another. Wildcards in verbs or resources, and any permission on nodes/proxy , deserve special review. Auditing List who can do something with kubectl auth can-i --list --as , or a plugin such as kubectl-who-can or rbac-tool to answer \"who can create pods\" across the cluster. Review ClusterRoleBindings to cluster-admin and system:masters regularly. The audit log shows which identities actually used permissions, which helps remove unused ones. kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | grep -B2 -A8 cluster-admin | head -40 Process Request access through a reviewed change in version control, grant it with an expiry where possible and remove it when the need ends. Review roles each quarter. Treat a new role that includes secrets, exec, escalate, bind or wildcards as needing a second reviewer. RBAC design, least privilege and auditing Flow in order: 1, Role or ClusterRole to Binding; 2, Binding to Group or user; 3, Group or user to Audit. Security Outside party Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Role or ClusterRole Security What it does: Defines permissions. If it fails or is weak: Wildcards. Binding Security What it does: Grants to subjects. If it fails or is weak: Cluster wide by mistake. Group or user Outside party What it does: Receives access. If it fails or is weak: Individuals. Audit Observability What it does: Finds excess. If it fails or is weak: Never reviewed. Incident: The read-only role that could escalate Incident A role called viewer turned out to let its holders become administrators. $ kubectl get clusterrole viewer -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.rules}&#x27; [{\"apiGroups\":[\"*\"],\"resources\":[\"pods\",\"secrets\"],\"verbs\":[\"get\",\"list\",\"create\"]}] $ kubectl auth can-i create pods -n kube-system --as viewer-user yes (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The role allowed create on pods and get on secrets across the cluster, so a holder could start a pod in any namespace, mount any secret or use any service account. The name hid the risk. Replacing it with a narrow role bound only in specific namespa"},"k8s-security/pod-security-standards-and-admission-policy":{"html":"\u003cp>A pod spec contains settings that decide how much of the node a container can touch. Pod Security Standards describe safe settings, and admission enforces them, so risky pods are stopped before they run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The three standards and what each forbids\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pod Security admission modes: enforce, audit and warn\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling out safely with labels per namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a compliant pod with a security context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy engines: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, Kyverno, Gatekeeper\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-standards\">The standards\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Privileged is unrestricted and meant for system components. Baseline blocks known escalations: privileged containers, host namespaces, host paths and unsafe capabilities. Restricted adds current hardening practice: run as non-root, no privilege escalation, drop all capabilities except a short list, a RuntimeDefault or Localhost seccomp profile and limited volume types.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"admission-modes\">Admission modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Pod Security admission controller is built in and configured with namespace labels. Mode enforce rejects violating pods, audit records violations in the audit log and warn returns a warning to the user. Each mode has a level and a version label.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest\nkubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted\nkubectl get ns shop --show-labels\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest\nkubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted\nkubectl get ns shop --show-labels\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rolling-out\">Rolling out\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Introduce it gradually. Label namespaces with warn and audit at the target level first and read the output, fix workloads, then switch on enforce. System namespaces such as kube-system usually need privileged. Because enforcement is per namespace, new namespaces need labels by default, which a platform can automate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-compliant-pod\">A compliant pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A restricted pod sets non-root, disables escalation, drops capabilities and uses the runtime default seccomp profile.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"securityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  runAsUser: 10001\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;]\n  seccompProfile:\n    type: RuntimeDefault\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsUser:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10001\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowPrivilegeEscalation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyRootFilesystem:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capabilities:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">drop:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeDefault\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"beyond-the-standards\">Beyond the standards\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The standards are fixed. Organisation rules such as allowed registries, required labels or banned host ports need a policy engine. ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, built in and written in CEL, covers many rules without running a webhook. Kyverno and OPA Gatekeeper add richer policy languages, mutation and reporting. Keep policy as code in version control and test it before rollout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ns&gt;psa;psa&gt;pol;pol&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Pod Security Standards and admission policy\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ns\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Namespace labels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"psa\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod Security admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod spec\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Namespace labels to Pod Security admission; 2, Pod Security admission to Policy engine; 3, Policy engine to Pod spec.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ns\">\u003cdt>Namespace labels \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Select level and mode.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"psa\">\u003cdt>Pod Security admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks each pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Warn only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Custom rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Webhook down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod spec \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Security context.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Root and escalation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-namespace-nobody-labelled\">Incident: A namespace nobody labelled\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A privileged pod ran in a new namespace because the standard was only applied to old ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get ns dev-new --show-labels\nkubectl get pod debug -n dev-new -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].securityContext}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ns dev-new --show-labels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME      STATUS   AGE   LABELS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dev-new   Active   2d    kubernetes.io/metadata.name=dev-new\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod debug -n dev-new -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[0].securityContext}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;privileged&quot;:true}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pod Security applies per namespace through labels, and the new namespace had none, so nothing was enforced. The team added a namespace creation workflow that sets warn, audit and enforce labels, and a policy that rejects namespaces without them. A control that must be remembered per namespace will eventually be forgotten.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enforcing restricted without a warn phase\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving new namespaces unlabelled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the standards for organisation specific rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a policy webhook without exclusions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting readOnlyRootFilesystem and capability drops\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-standards","text":"The standards"},{"id":"admission-modes","text":"Admission modes"},{"id":"rolling-out","text":"Rolling out"},{"id":"a-compliant-pod","text":"A compliant pod"},{"id":"beyond-the-standards","text":"Beyond the standards"},{"id":"incident-a-namespace-nobody-labelled","text":"Incident: A namespace nobody labelled"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pod Security Standards","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"},{"t":"Validating admission policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/validating-admission-policy/"}],"text":"A pod spec contains settings that decide how much of the node a container can touch. Pod Security Standards describe safe settings, and admission enforces them, so risky pods are stopped before they run. What you will learn The three standards and what each forbids Pod Security admission modes: enforce, audit and warn Rolling out safely with labels per namespace Writing a compliant pod with a security context Policy engines: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, Kyverno, Gatekeeper The standards Privileged is unrestricted and meant for system components. Baseline blocks known escalations: privileged containers, host namespaces, host paths and unsafe capabilities. Restricted adds current hardening practice: run as non-root, no privilege escalation, drop all capabilities except a short list, a RuntimeDefault or Localhost seccomp profile and limited volume types. Admission modes The Pod Security admission controller is built in and configured with namespace labels. Mode enforce rejects violating pods, audit records violations in the audit log and warn returns a warning to the user. Each mode has a level and a version label. kubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest kubectl label ns shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted kubectl get ns shop --show-labels Rolling out Introduce it gradually. Label namespaces with warn and audit at the target level first and read the output, fix workloads, then switch on enforce. System namespaces such as kube-system usually need privileged. Because enforcement is per namespace, new namespaces need labels by default, which a platform can automate. A compliant pod A restricted pod sets non-root, disables escalation, drops capabilities and uses the runtime default seccomp profile. securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true runAsUser: 10001 allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: drop: [ \"ALL\" ] seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault Beyond the standards The standards are fixed. Organisation rules such as allowed registries, required labels or banned host ports need a policy engine. ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, built in and written in CEL, covers many rules without running a webhook. Kyverno and OPA Gatekeeper add richer policy languages, mutation and reporting. Keep policy as code in version control and test it before rollout. Pod Security Standards and admission policy Flow in order: 1, Namespace labels to Pod Security admission; 2, Pod Security admission to Policy engine; 3, Policy engine to Pod spec. Data store Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Namespace labels Data store What it does: Select level and mode. If it fails or is weak: Missing labels. Pod Security admission Security What it does: Checks each pod. If it fails or is weak: Warn only. Policy engine Security What it does: Custom rules. If it fails or is weak: Webhook down. Pod spec Step What it does: Security context. If it fails or is weak: Root and escalation. Incident: A namespace nobody labelled Incident A privileged pod ran in a new namespace because the standard was only applied to old ones. $ kubectl get ns dev-new --show-labels NAME STATUS AGE LABELS dev-new Active 2d kubernetes.io/metadata.name=dev-new $ kubectl get pod debug -n dev-new -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[0].securityContext}&#x27; {\"privileged\":true} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Pod Security applies per namespace through labels, and the new namespace had none, so nothing was enforced. The team added a namespace creation workflow that sets warn, audit and enforce labels, and a policy that rejects namespaces without them. A control that must be remembered per namespace will eventually be forgotten. Common mistakes Enforcing restricted without a warn phase Leaving new namespaces unlabelled Using the standards "},"k8s-security/secrets-encryption-at-rest-and-external-stores":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes Secrets are base64 encoded, not encrypted. Out of the box anyone who can read etcd, a backup or the Secret object itself can read the value. Several layers fix that, and each addresses a different threat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a Secret protects and what it does not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encryption at rest with an EncryptionConfiguration and KMS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access control and avoiding exposure through pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>External secret stores and the CSI driver or operators\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotation after exposure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-base64-gives-you\">What base64 gives you\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Secret stores values as base64 so that binary data fits in YAML. Decoding takes one command. The protection comes from RBAC on the API, from encryption of the stored bytes and from keeping Secrets out of places where they leak, such as logs, environment dumps, images and version control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"encryption-at-rest\">Encryption at rest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The API server can encrypt chosen resources before writing them to etcd, using an EncryptionConfiguration file passed with \u003ccode>--encryption-provider-config\u003c/code>. Providers include aescbc and secretbox with a local key, and kms, which wraps a data key with a key in an external key management service, so the key never sits next to the data. The first provider in the list is used to write, and the others are for reading. After enabling it, rewrite existing Secrets so they are encrypted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\nkind: EncryptionConfiguration\nresources:\n  - resources: [secrets]\n    providers:\n      - aescbc:\n          keys:\n            - name: key1\n              secret: BASE64_32_BYTE_KEY\n      - identity: {}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">EncryptionConfiguration\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">secrets\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">providers:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aescbc:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">keys:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">key1\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secret:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">BASE64_32_BYTE_KEY\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">identity:\u003c/span> {}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify\">Verify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read a Secret directly from etcd and check for the encryption prefix instead of readable text. An encrypted value starts with \u003ccode>k8s:enc:\u003c/code> and the provider name.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/shop/db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | head -c 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/shop/db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 80\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reducing-exposure\">Reducing exposure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restrict get, list and watch on Secrets, since list returns every value. Mount Secrets as files instead of environment variables when possible, because environment variables appear in process listings and crash dumps. Limit who can create pods in a namespace, because a pod can mount any Secret there. Never put Secrets in images or manifests in version control unencrypted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"external-stores\">External stores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tools such as the External Secrets Operator and the Secrets Store CSI driver fetch values from a manager such as Vault or a cloud secrets service and present them to pods, with central rotation, audit and access policy. They reduce the amount of long lived material in etcd. The trade is dependency on the external service and its availability. After any exposure, rotate the secret itself and everything that used it, since removing access does not undo a copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;api;api&gt;etcd;api&gt;kms\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Secrets: encryption at rest and external stores\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kms\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">KMS or external store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod to API server; 2, API server to etcd; 3, API server to KMS or external store.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Mounts the secret.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Env var leak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypts on write.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores ciphertext.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plain text.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kms\">\u003cdt>KMS or external store \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unavailable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-plain-text-in-a-backup\">Incident: Plain text in a backup\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A routine review found a database password readable in an etcd snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"strings /backup/etcd-snapshot.db | grep -m1 -i password\ngrep -c encryption-provider-config /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>strings /backup/etcd-snapshot.db | grep -m1 -i password\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">password: Sup3rS3cret\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c encryption-provider-config /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without an encryption configuration, Secrets were stored in readable form, and snapshots, stored on a shared disk, exposed them. The team enabled encryption at rest, rewrote all Secrets, rotated the exposed credentials and encrypted and restricted backups. Enabling encryption protects new writes only until existing objects are rewritten.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Believing base64 is encryption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enabling encryption and not rewriting existing Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting list on Secrets broadly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing secrets as environment variables everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not rotating after exposure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-base64-gives-you","text":"What base64 gives you"},{"id":"encryption-at-rest","text":"Encryption at rest"},{"id":"verify","text":"Verify"},{"id":"reducing-exposure","text":"Reducing exposure"},{"id":"external-stores","text":"External stores"},{"id":"incident-plain-text-in-a-backup","text":"Incident: Plain text in a backup"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Encrypting confidential data at rest","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/"},{"t":"Good practices for Kubernetes Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/secrets-good-practices/"}],"text":"Kubernetes Secrets are base64 encoded, not encrypted. Out of the box anyone who can read etcd, a backup or the Secret object itself can read the value. Several layers fix that, and each addresses a different threat. What you will learn What a Secret protects and what it does not Encryption at rest with an EncryptionConfiguration and KMS Access control and avoiding exposure through pods External secret stores and the CSI driver or operators Rotation after exposure What base64 gives you A Secret stores values as base64 so that binary data fits in YAML. Decoding takes one command. The protection comes from RBAC on the API, from encryption of the stored bytes and from keeping Secrets out of places where they leak, such as logs, environment dumps, images and version control. Encryption at rest The API server can encrypt chosen resources before writing them to etcd, using an EncryptionConfiguration file passed with --encryption-provider-config . Providers include aescbc and secretbox with a local key, and kms, which wraps a data key with a key in an external key management service, so the key never sits next to the data. The first provider in the list is used to write, and the others are for reading. After enabling it, rewrite existing Secrets so they are encrypted. apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1 kind: EncryptionConfiguration resources: - resources: [ secrets ] providers: - aescbc: keys: - name: key1 secret: BASE64_32_BYTE_KEY - identity: {} kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f - Verify Read a Secret directly from etcd and check for the encryption prefix instead of readable text. An encrypted value starts with k8s:enc: and the provider name. ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/shop/db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | head -c 80 Reducing exposure Restrict get, list and watch on Secrets, since list returns every value. Mount Secrets as files instead of environment variables when possible, because environment variables appear in process listings and crash dumps. Limit who can create pods in a namespace, because a pod can mount any Secret there. Never put Secrets in images or manifests in version control unencrypted. External stores Tools such as the External Secrets Operator and the Secrets Store CSI driver fetch values from a manager such as Vault or a cloud secrets service and present them to pods, with central rotation, audit and access policy. They reduce the amount of long lived material in etcd. The trade is dependency on the external service and its availability. After any exposure, rotate the secret itself and everything that used it, since removing access does not undo a copy. Secrets: encryption at rest and external stores Flow in order: 1, Pod to API server; 2, API server to etcd; 3, API server to KMS or external store. Step Service Data store Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod Step What it does: Mounts the secret. If it fails or is weak: Env var leak. API server Service What it does: Encrypts on write. If it fails or is weak: No config. etcd Data store What it does: Stores ciphertext. If it fails or is weak: Plain text. KMS or external store Outside party What it does: Holds the key. If it fails or is weak: Unavailable. Incident: Plain text in a backup Incident A routine review found a database password readable in an etcd snapshot. $ strings /backup/etcd-snapshot.db | grep -m1 -i password password: Sup3rS3cret $ grep -c encryption-provider-config /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Without an encryption configuration, Secrets were stored in readable form, and snapshots, stored on a shared disk, exposed them. The team enabled encryption at rest, rewrote all Secrets, rotated the exposed credentials and en"},"k8s-security/multi-tenancy-and-isolation":{"html":"\u003cp>Sharing a cluster saves money and effort, but it shares the kernel, the control plane and the network. How much isolation you add depends on how much you trust the tenants, and the options form a ladder.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Soft and hard multi-tenancy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Namespaces as the unit and what they do not isolate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Node pools, sandboxed runtimes and virtual clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolating network, secrets, resources and policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing the level\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"soft-and-hard\">Soft and hard\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Soft multi-tenancy assumes tenants are cooperating teams in one organisation who might make mistakes. Hard multi-tenancy assumes a tenant may be hostile, as in a service offered to outside customers. Kubernetes can give strong soft isolation easily. Hard isolation inside one cluster is difficult because containers share a kernel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"namespaces\">Namespaces\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A namespace scopes names, RBAC, quotas and policies. It does not by itself isolate the network, nodes or kernel. Pair each tenant namespace with a RoleBinding to the tenant group, a ResourceQuota and LimitRange, default deny network policies, Pod Security labels and no access to other tenants Secrets. Disable service account token automount and cross namespace service access where not needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stronger-isolation\">Stronger isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dedicated node pools with taints and node selectors keep tenant pods off shared nodes and limit the effect of a container escape. Sandboxed runtimes such as gVisor or Kata Containers, selected with a RuntimeClass, add a stronger boundary between the container and the host kernel. Virtual clusters give each tenant its own API server and control plane objects on shared nodes, reducing control plane sharing. Separate clusters give the strongest isolation at the highest cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RuntimeClass\nmetadata:\n  name: sandboxed\nhandler: runsc\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeClass\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sandboxed\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">handler:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">runsc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-components\">Shared components\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch the things that are shared: cluster scoped resources such as CRDs and ClusterRoles, admission webhooks, the DNS and ingress controllers, logging and monitoring, and the API server itself, where a noisy tenant can use up request capacity. Use API Priority and Fairness settings, quotas on object counts and per-tenant observability.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For teams in one company, namespaces with the controls above are usually enough. For regulated or semi-trusted workloads, add node pools and a sandbox runtime. For untrusted code from outside parties, prefer separate clusters or hardware isolation. Document the model so that tenants know what they share.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"ns&gt;net;ns&gt;node;node&gt;run;run&gt;cl\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Multi-tenancy and isolation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ns\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Namespace controls\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network isolation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sandbox runtime\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Separate cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Namespace controls to Network isolation; 2, Namespace controls to Node pool; 3, Node pool to Sandbox runtime; 4, Sandbox runtime to Separate cluster.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ns\">\u003cdt>Namespace controls \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RBAC, quota, policy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network isolation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Default deny.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Open by default.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dedicated nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Sandbox runtime \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Kernel boundary.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not enabled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Separate cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Strongest isolation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-neighbour-that-starved-the-api-server\">Incident: A neighbour that starved the API server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>One tenant controller listing every pod in a tight loop made other tenants deployments slow.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_current_inqueue_requests | head -3\nkubectl get flowschemas | grep tenant\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_current_inqueue_requests | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">apiserver_flowcontrol_current_inqueue_requests{priority_level=&quot;workload-low&quot;} 164\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get flowschemas | grep tenant\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no output)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tenant tool shared a default flow with everyone else, and its heavy list calls queued other requests behind it. Adding a flow schema that placed the tenant in its own priority level, and fixing the tool to use a watch, restored fair service. Shared control plane capacity is a multi-tenancy concern, not only pods and networks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating namespaces as a security boundary on their own\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Missing default deny network policy per tenant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing nodes for untrusted workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring noisy neighbours on the API server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not documenting what is shared\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"soft-and-hard","text":"Soft and hard"},{"id":"namespaces","text":"Namespaces"},{"id":"stronger-isolation","text":"Stronger isolation"},{"id":"shared-components","text":"Shared components"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-a-neighbour-that-starved-the-api-server","text":"Incident: A neighbour that starved the API server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Multi-tenancy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/multi-tenancy/"},{"t":"Runtime class","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/runtime-class/"}],"text":"Sharing a cluster saves money and effort, but it shares the kernel, the control plane and the network. How much isolation you add depends on how much you trust the tenants, and the options form a ladder. What you will learn Soft and hard multi-tenancy Namespaces as the unit and what they do not isolate Node pools, sandboxed runtimes and virtual clusters Isolating network, secrets, resources and policy Choosing the level Soft and hard Soft multi-tenancy assumes tenants are cooperating teams in one organisation who might make mistakes. Hard multi-tenancy assumes a tenant may be hostile, as in a service offered to outside customers. Kubernetes can give strong soft isolation easily. Hard isolation inside one cluster is difficult because containers share a kernel. Namespaces A namespace scopes names, RBAC, quotas and policies. It does not by itself isolate the network, nodes or kernel. Pair each tenant namespace with a RoleBinding to the tenant group, a ResourceQuota and LimitRange, default deny network policies, Pod Security labels and no access to other tenants Secrets. Disable service account token automount and cross namespace service access where not needed. Stronger isolation Dedicated node pools with taints and node selectors keep tenant pods off shared nodes and limit the effect of a container escape. Sandboxed runtimes such as gVisor or Kata Containers, selected with a RuntimeClass, add a stronger boundary between the container and the host kernel. Virtual clusters give each tenant its own API server and control plane objects on shared nodes, reducing control plane sharing. Separate clusters give the strongest isolation at the highest cost. apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1 kind: RuntimeClass metadata: name: sandboxed handler: runsc Shared components Watch the things that are shared: cluster scoped resources such as CRDs and ClusterRoles, admission webhooks, the DNS and ingress controllers, logging and monitoring, and the API server itself, where a noisy tenant can use up request capacity. Use API Priority and Fairness settings, quotas on object counts and per-tenant observability. Choosing For teams in one company, namespaces with the controls above are usually enough. For regulated or semi-trusted workloads, add node pools and a sandbox runtime. For untrusted code from outside parties, prefer separate clusters or hardware isolation. Document the model so that tenants know what they share. Multi-tenancy and isolation Flow in order: 1, Namespace controls to Network isolation; 2, Namespace controls to Node pool; 3, Node pool to Sandbox runtime; 4, Sandbox runtime to Separate cluster. Security Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Namespace controls Security What it does: RBAC, quota, policy. If it fails or is weak: Missing one. Network isolation Security What it does: Default deny. If it fails or is weak: Open by default. Node pool Step What it does: Dedicated nodes. If it fails or is weak: Shared nodes. Sandbox runtime Security What it does: Kernel boundary. If it fails or is weak: Not enabled. Separate cluster Service What it does: Strongest isolation. If it fails or is weak: Cost. Incident: A neighbour that starved the API server Incident One tenant controller listing every pod in a tight loop made other tenants deployments slow. $ kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_current_inqueue_requests | head -3 apiserver_flowcontrol_current_inqueue_requests{priority_level=\"workload-low\"} 164 $ kubectl get flowschemas | grep tenant (no output) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The tenant tool shared a default flow with everyone else, and its heavy list calls queued other requests behind it. Adding a flow schema that placed the tenant in its own priority level, and fixing the tool to use a watch, restored fair service. Shared control plane capacity is a multi-tenancy concern, not only pods and networks. Common "},"k8s-security/api-server-and-kubelet-hardening-with-cis":{"html":"\u003cp>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a long list of configuration checks. Tools run it for you, but you still have to understand a finding well enough to fix it safely, because the control plane settings live in static pod manifests where a typo stops the API server.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to run kube-bench and read its output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Important API server flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kubelet settings that matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>etcd and file permission checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A safe edit procedure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-the-benchmark\">Running the benchmark\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kube-bench is an open source tool that runs the checks as a job or on the node and prints PASS, FAIL, WARN and INFO for each numbered recommendation, with a remediation text. Run it on control plane and worker nodes, because they have different checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kube-bench run --targets master,node,etcd,policies\nkube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kube-bench run --targets master,node,etcd,policies\nkube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api-server-settings\">API server settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Key settings include anonymous authentication off, authorization mode Node and RBAC, never AlwaysAllow, the NodeRestriction admission plugin enabled, audit logging on with a policy, profiling off, TLS cipher and version settings, the service account issuer and key files set, and etcd client certificates used. Insecure ports must be closed. The manifest at \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\u003c/code> holds these as command flags.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubelet-settings\">Kubelet settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On each node the kubelet configuration should set \u003ccode>authentication.anonymous.enabled: false\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>authorization.mode: Webhook\u003c/code>, a client CA file for x509 authentication, \u003ccode>readOnlyPort: 0\u003c/code> to disable the unauthenticated read-only port, \u003ccode>protectKernelDefaults: true\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>rotateCertificates: true\u003c/code> and event rate limits. A kubelet that accepts anonymous requests with AlwaysAllow lets anyone who can reach port 10250 run commands in pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -E &quot;anonymous|mode|readOnlyPort|protectKernelDefaults&quot; /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\nsudo systemctl restart kubelet\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;anonymous|mode|readOnlyPort|protectKernelDefaults&quot;\u003c/span> /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart kubelet\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"etcd-and-files\">etcd and files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>etcd must use client certificate authentication, peer TLS, and a data directory owned by etcd with mode 700. Configuration files and certificates under \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes\u003c/code> should be owned by root with restrictive permissions, and the kubeconfig files not world readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-safe-procedure\">A safe procedure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copy the manifest before editing, change one flag at a time, then watch the static pod restart with \u003ccode>crictl ps\u003c/code> and check that \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code> still works. If the API server does not return, read its container logs and restore the copy. Re-run the single failed check afterward to confirm the fix, and keep a record of exceptions you accept with their reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"run&gt;api;run&gt;kl;run&gt;etcd;api&gt;re;kl&gt;re\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">API server and kubelet hardening with the CIS benchmark\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kube-bench\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server flags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kl\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubelet config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd and files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"re\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Re-run check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kube-bench to API server flags; 2, kube-bench to Kubelet config; 3, kube-bench to etcd and files; 4, API server flags to Re-run check; 5, Kubelet config to Re-run check.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>kube-bench \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reports findings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong target.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server flags \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Auth, audit, admission.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Typo in manifest.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kl\">\u003cdt>Kubelet config \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Anonymous off.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not restarted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd and files \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> TLS and permissions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> World readable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"re\">\u003cdt>Re-run check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prove the fix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-audit-flag-that-stopped-the-api-server\">Incident: The audit flag that stopped the API server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer enabled audit logging and the API server did not return.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver\nsudo crictl logs f3a1c9 2&gt;&amp;1 | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">f3a1c9  Exited  kube-apiserver  4 minutes ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl logs f3a1c9 2&gt;&amp;1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: loading audit policy file: open /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml: no such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The flags were added, but the policy file was not mounted into the static pod, so the process exited at start and the kubelet kept restarting it. Restoring the backed up manifest recovered the API, and the engineer re-applied the change with the volume and mount for the policy and log paths. Always copy the manifest first, and remember that files used by the API server must be mounted into the pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing a manifest without a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding file flags without volume mounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing many findings at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running only the control plane checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Accepting findings without recording the reason\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"running-the-benchmark","text":"Running the benchmark"},{"id":"api-server-settings","text":"API server settings"},{"id":"kubelet-settings","text":"Kubelet settings"},{"id":"etcd-and-files","text":"etcd and files"},{"id":"a-safe-procedure","text":"A safe procedure"},{"id":"incident-the-audit-flag-that-stopped-the-api-server","text":"Incident: The audit flag that stopped the API server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CIS Kubernetes Benchmark","u":"https://www.cisecurity.org/benchmark/kubernetes"},{"t":"Securing a cluster","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/securing-a-cluster/"}],"text":"The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a long list of configuration checks. Tools run it for you, but you still have to understand a finding well enough to fix it safely, because the control plane settings live in static pod manifests where a typo stops the API server. What you will learn How to run kube-bench and read its output Important API server flags Kubelet settings that matter etcd and file permission checks A safe edit procedure Running the benchmark kube-bench is an open source tool that runs the checks as a job or on the node and prints PASS, FAIL, WARN and INFO for each numbered recommendation, with a remediation text. Run it on control plane and worker nodes, because they have different checks. kube-bench run --targets master,node,etcd,policies kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1 API server settings Key settings include anonymous authentication off, authorization mode Node and RBAC, never AlwaysAllow, the NodeRestriction admission plugin enabled, audit logging on with a policy, profiling off, TLS cipher and version settings, the service account issuer and key files set, and etcd client certificates used. Insecure ports must be closed. The manifest at /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml holds these as command flags. Kubelet settings On each node the kubelet configuration should set authentication.anonymous.enabled: false , authorization.mode: Webhook , a client CA file for x509 authentication, readOnlyPort: 0 to disable the unauthenticated read-only port, protectKernelDefaults: true , rotateCertificates: true and event rate limits. A kubelet that accepts anonymous requests with AlwaysAllow lets anyone who can reach port 10250 run commands in pods. grep -E \"anonymous|mode|readOnlyPort|protectKernelDefaults\" /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml sudo systemctl restart kubelet etcd and files etcd must use client certificate authentication, peer TLS, and a data directory owned by etcd with mode 700. Configuration files and certificates under /etc/kubernetes should be owned by root with restrictive permissions, and the kubeconfig files not world readable. A safe procedure Copy the manifest before editing, change one flag at a time, then watch the static pod restart with crictl ps and check that kubectl get nodes still works. If the API server does not return, read its container logs and restore the copy. Re-run the single failed check afterward to confirm the fix, and keep a record of exceptions you accept with their reason. API server and kubelet hardening with the CIS benchmark Flow in order: 1, kube-bench to API server flags; 2, kube-bench to Kubelet config; 3, kube-bench to etcd and files; 4, API server flags to Re-run check; 5, Kubelet config to Re-run check. Observability Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. kube-bench Observability What it does: Reports findings. If it fails or is weak: Wrong target. API server flags Security What it does: Auth, audit, admission. If it fails or is weak: Typo in manifest. Kubelet config Security What it does: Anonymous off. If it fails or is weak: Not restarted. etcd and files Security What it does: TLS and permissions. If it fails or is weak: World readable. Re-run check Observability What it does: Prove the fix. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Incident: The audit flag that stopped the API server Incident An engineer enabled audit logging and the API server did not return. $ sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver f3a1c9 Exited kube-apiserver 4 minutes ago $ sudo crictl logs f3a1c9 2>&1 | tail -2 Error: loading audit policy file: open /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml: no such file or directory (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The flags were added, but the policy file was not mounted into the static pod, so the process exited at start and the kubelet kept restarting it. Restoring the backed up manifest recovered the API, and the engineer re-applied the change with the"},"k8s-security/incident-response-for-a-compromised-cluster":{"html":"\u003cp>When a workload is compromised, the pressure is to delete it. Deleting destroys evidence and may leave the real problem untouched. A short, practised order of actions contains the damage first and investigates second.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Signals: audit logs, Falco alerts, network and cost anomalies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Containment without destroying evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Investigation steps and what to collect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Eradication and credential rotation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lessons and detection improvements\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detection\">Detection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Signals come from several places. Falco or another runtime sensor alerts on a shell spawned in a container, writes below /etc, unexpected outbound connections and reads of sensitive files. The audit log shows unusual API calls, such as listing secrets or creating privileged pods from a service account that normally does not. Cost or network graphs may show cryptomining or data exfiltration. Treat an alert as a hypothesis and confirm it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contain-first\">Contain first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Limit the blast radius before you investigate. Apply a network policy that isolates the pod, label it so a quarantine policy matches, and cordon the node so nothing new lands there. Revoke or rotate the service account token and any credential the pod could read. Preserve evidence: do not delete the pod yet. Take a snapshot of the node disk if you can, and copy logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label pod web-7d9 quarantine=true --overwrite\nkubectl cordon node-a3\nkubectl get pod web-7d9 -o yaml &gt; /secure/evidence/web-7d9.yaml\nkubectl logs web-7d9 --all-containers &gt; /secure/evidence/web-7d9.log\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label pod web-7d9 quarantine=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span> --overwrite\nkubectl cordon node-a3\nkubectl get pod web-7d9 -o yaml &gt; /secure/evidence/web-7d9.yaml\nkubectl logs web-7d9 --all-containers &gt; /secure/evidence/web-7d9.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"investigate\">Investigate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Find out how the attacker got in, what they touched and whether they moved. Review audit logs for that service account and for the node, list what the pod could access with its permissions, check for new pods, jobs, cron jobs and RBAC objects, inspect the image and the running process tree, and check cloud audit logs for the node role. Build a timeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"eradicate-and-recover\">Eradicate and recover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Remove the cause: patch the vulnerability, rebuild the image, redeploy from a known good source and replace affected nodes instead of cleaning them. Rotate every credential that was reachable: tokens, Secrets, database passwords and cloud keys. Verify with scans and detection that the attacker no longer has access, and watch closely for a while.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"learn\">Learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a short blameless review. Add the detection that would have caught the event earlier, close the path that was used, and rehearse the response. Keep a runbook with contacts, decisions about who may isolate production workloads and where evidence is stored.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"det&gt;cont;cont&gt;inv;inv&gt;rec;rec&gt;lrn\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Incident response for a compromised cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"det\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Detect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cont\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Contain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Investigate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rec\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lrn\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Learn\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Detect to Contain; 2, Contain to Investigate; 3, Investigate to Recover; 4, Recover to Learn.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"det\">\u003cdt>Detect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Falco, audit, graphs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not alerting.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cont\">\u003cdt>Contain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Isolate and rotate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Delete too early.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Investigate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Timeline and scope.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Partial view.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rec\">\u003cdt>Recover \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rebuild and rotate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reused credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lrn\">\u003cdt>Learn \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> New detection.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No review.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-deleting-the-pod-and-losing-the-trail\">Incident: Deleting the pod and losing the trail\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A suspicious pod was deleted immediately, and the team could not tell what it had accessed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl delete pod web-7d9\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl logs web-7d9\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl delete pod web-7d9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pod &quot;web-7d9&quot; deleted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l app=web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME      READY   STATUS    AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-8c1   1/1     Running   4s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs web-7d9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server (NotFound): pods &quot;web-7d9&quot; not found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The replacement pod appeared at once, but the original process tree, files and logs were gone, and its service account token was still valid. The team could not tell what the attacker had read. In a later incident they isolated the pod with a network policy, rotated the token, copied evidence and then replaced it. Contain first, preserve evidence, then remove.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting the workload before collecting evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not rotating the credentials the pod could reach\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning a node instead of replacing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No runtime detection or audit logs enabled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No written response runbook\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"detection","text":"Detection"},{"id":"contain-first","text":"Contain first"},{"id":"investigate","text":"Investigate"},{"id":"eradicate-and-recover","text":"Eradicate and recover"},{"id":"learn","text":"Learn"},{"id":"incident-deleting-the-pod-and-losing-the-trail","text":"Incident: Deleting the pod and losing the trail"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Auditing","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit/"},{"t":"Kubernetes security checklist","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/security-checklist/"}],"text":"When a workload is compromised, the pressure is to delete it. Deleting destroys evidence and may leave the real problem untouched. A short, practised order of actions contains the damage first and investigates second. What you will learn Signals: audit logs, Falco alerts, network and cost anomalies Containment without destroying evidence Investigation steps and what to collect Eradication and credential rotation Lessons and detection improvements Detection Signals come from several places. Falco or another runtime sensor alerts on a shell spawned in a container, writes below /etc, unexpected outbound connections and reads of sensitive files. The audit log shows unusual API calls, such as listing secrets or creating privileged pods from a service account that normally does not. Cost or network graphs may show cryptomining or data exfiltration. Treat an alert as a hypothesis and confirm it. Contain first Limit the blast radius before you investigate. Apply a network policy that isolates the pod, label it so a quarantine policy matches, and cordon the node so nothing new lands there. Revoke or rotate the service account token and any credential the pod could read. Preserve evidence: do not delete the pod yet. Take a snapshot of the node disk if you can, and copy logs. kubectl label pod web-7d9 quarantine= true --overwrite kubectl cordon node-a3 kubectl get pod web-7d9 -o yaml > /secure/evidence/web-7d9.yaml kubectl logs web-7d9 --all-containers > /secure/evidence/web-7d9. log Investigate Find out how the attacker got in, what they touched and whether they moved. Review audit logs for that service account and for the node, list what the pod could access with its permissions, check for new pods, jobs, cron jobs and RBAC objects, inspect the image and the running process tree, and check cloud audit logs for the node role. Build a timeline. Eradicate and recover Remove the cause: patch the vulnerability, rebuild the image, redeploy from a known good source and replace affected nodes instead of cleaning them. Rotate every credential that was reachable: tokens, Secrets, database passwords and cloud keys. Verify with scans and detection that the attacker no longer has access, and watch closely for a while. Learn Write a short blameless review. Add the detection that would have caught the event earlier, close the path that was used, and rehearse the response. Keep a runbook with contacts, decisions about who may isolate production workloads and where evidence is stored. Incident response for a compromised cluster Flow in order: 1, Detect to Contain; 2, Contain to Investigate; 3, Investigate to Recover; 4, Recover to Learn. Observability Security Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Detect Observability What it does: Falco, audit, graphs. If it fails or is weak: Not alerting. Contain Security What it does: Isolate and rotate. If it fails or is weak: Delete too early. Investigate Observability What it does: Timeline and scope. If it fails or is weak: Partial view. Recover Step What it does: Rebuild and rotate. If it fails or is weak: Reused credentials. Learn Data store What it does: New detection. If it fails or is weak: No review. Incident: Deleting the pod and losing the trail Incident A suspicious pod was deleted immediately, and the team could not tell what it had accessed. $ kubectl delete pod web-7d9 pod \"web-7d9\" deleted $ kubectl get pods -l app=web NAME READY STATUS AGE web-8c1 1/1 Running 4s $ kubectl logs web-7d9 Error from server (NotFound): pods \"web-7d9\" not found (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The replacement pod appeared at once, but the original process tree, files and logs were gone, and its service account token was still valid. The team could not tell what the attacker had read. In a later incident they isolated the pod with a network policy, rotated the token, copied evidence and then replaced it. Cont"}});